; resettest.eval — does `reset` really equal a new game?
;
; Run:  Dungeon.exe -eval tools\EvalScripts\resettest.eval
;
; THIS IS THE CHECK THE WHOLE RECYCLING IDEA RESTS ON. `reset` exists so a long
; run can skip the ~12-second level load between tests, and it is defined as
; "put the world where `newgame` would". That definition was chosen BECAUSE it
; can be tested: take a baseline after a real new game, wreck the world every way
; the harness knows how, reset, and take the baseline again. The two blocks must
; match line for line.
;
; WHY IT MATTERS MORE THAN IT LOOKS. An incomplete reset does not crash and does
; not print anything wrong — it quietly contaminates every later test in the run,
; and their numbers stay entirely plausible. This harness has already been bitten
; by exactly that shape once: the m_partyWiped latch survived a heal, and twelve
; ladder rungs reported 45 simulated seconds of nothing at all.
;
; READ IT BY DIFFING the two `=== BASELINE` blocks. Anything that differs is
; something `reset` failed to put back. (The RNG seed is deliberately NOT reset —
; it is how a run is DRIVEN, not what the world contains — so the script reseeds
; either side, exactly as a real suite would.)

logecho on
reset
timescale 0
lockstep on

; --- the baseline, after a real new game -------------------------------------
seed 7
; The FIRST `reset` above was a real load — nothing was loaded to recycle — so
; this baseline is a genuine new game, and the one at the bottom is a recycle.
; That is the comparison, and it is the same one either way round.
echo === BASELINE A (after a real load) ===
state
party
supplies
regen
monsters
char 0
; THE MAP, and it is here because leaving it out is how the first version of
; this test passed on a world that was still an empty carved box. `arena` walls
; every cell and strips the map's fixtures, stairs and decorations; none of the
; probes above would show any of that. `mapinfo` prints the counts, and a
; walkability sample proves the CELLS came back and not just the records.
mapinfo

; --- now wreck it, every way the harness can ---------------------------------
echo === wrecking the world ===
arena open 9 9
spawn skel_warrior 14 11
spawn skel_warrior 15 12
effect bleed 0 6 5
effect bleed 1 6 5
setstat 2 vitality 20
setskill 3 fire 9
setskill 0 conditioning 12
setsupply all food 20
setsupply all water 15
give waterskin 1
learn 3 fire
autoattack on
freeze on
tally reset
forward 3
step 8
cast 3 fire
step 2
echo --- wrecked ---
party
supplies
monsters
tally

; --- and put it back ----------------------------------------------------------
reset
seed 7
echo === BASELINE B (after reset) ===
state
party
supplies
regen
monsters
char 0
; THE MAP, and it is here because leaving it out is how the first version of
; this test passed on a world that was still an empty carved box. `arena` walls
; every cell and strips the map's fixtures, stairs and decorations; none of the
; probes above would show any of that. `mapinfo` prints the counts, and a
; walkability sample proves the CELLS came back and not just the records.
mapinfo
; NOTHING after this line. The two baselines are compared line for line, so a
; trailing readout here would make block B one longer than block A and fail the
; check for a reason that has nothing to do with the reset.
